  "query_preview": "..Radar and satellite imagery show showers and\nthunderstorm activity is still mostly within Yavapai and northern\nGila counties, while stratiform rain, with isolated embedded \nthunderstorms moving through Coconino and into southern Navajo and\nApache counties, mainly along and south of the I-40 corridor. \nSome lighter showers can be found north of the I-40 corridor, but \nthe focus for heavier rainfall is along and south of the Mogollon \nRim, and especially western AZ, where instability and moisture \ntransport ahead of the shortwave trough is the greatest. \nWidespread rainfall totals of 0.25 to 0.50 inches of rain, with \nlocally heavier amounts of around 1 inch are still possible for \nYavapai and northern Gila counties through the rest of the today, \nespecially in areas that experience a heavy thunderstorm or \nseveral rounds of showers, keeping the Flash Flood Watch in effect\nfor both counties through 10 PM MST this evening.\n\nAs the shortwave trough moves off to the east this evening, the\nforcing, instability and shear over southern and central AZ will\ndecrease fairly quickly. Forecast trends continue to support that\nshowers will be isolated Saturday morning through early Saturday \nevening, limiting the risk for flooding, with partly sunny to \nmostly cloudy skies through the day.\n\nBy later Saturday evening, the next Pacific system along the west\ncoast will start to move southeastward as a significant plume of \nsub-tropical moisture is pulled into the Four Corners region and a\nshortwave trough passes south of AZ. This air mass will be near \nrecords as far as PW is concerned for this time of year and the \nphasing of the two systems will allow for an extended period of \nmoderate to heavy rainfall.\n\nThe threat for severe weather with the second system remains very\nlimit, thanks largely to a fully saturated and somewhat cold \ncolumn over the state of Arizona. This will limit the\nredevelopment of instability ahead of the main system. However, \nlater on Sunday and Sunday night when the parent trough moves \nthrough, there could be some isolated severe along the cold front.\n\nThe period of precipitation for the second event will stretch from \nSaturday evening through Monday morning, allowing plenty of time for \ntotals to add up. The sweet spot, as of now, looks to be along and\nsouth of the Mogollon Rim/east of the I-17 corridor, where 2-4 \ninches of rainfall and locally higher amounts could fall with the\nheavier bands. Even within the areas that are not as phased and \nnot seeing as much of an upslope component, as much as 2 inches \nmay accumulate. This is not including the rainfall that fall today,\nso more than enough to cause serious hydrological issues,\nespecially areas that see rain today. A second FFA is in effect \nfor the entire CWA during this time frame.\n\nBehind the final trough passage Monday morning, we quickly dry out \nwith strong ridging developing for a few days. Long-range\nensembles are hinting at another cut-off low forming near the end\nof next week/weekend, potentially bring another round of showers\nand thunderstorms.\n\nThis is a dangerous situation, especially pertaining to flooding. \nBoth flash flooding and then longer term river flooding will be a \nproblem. Larger stem waterways may be flooding for days after the \nevent, especially the Little Colorado River and the Verde River.",
